      "text": "We know that $\\sin \\theta = \\sin (\\theta + 2\\pi)$ for all $\\theta.$ In our problem, we know that $\\frac{11 \\pi}{3}<2 \\pi.$ We can write $\\frac{11 \\pi}{3}=2 \\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{3},$ so\n\\[\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3} = \\sin \\left(2 \\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin \\left(\\pi + \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right).\\]Since the sine function has period $\\pi,$ and since $\\pi < \\frac{5\\pi}{3},$ we have\n\\[\\sin \\left(\\pi + \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}.\\]. The answer is -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}",
