    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We start by expressing $\\frac{11 \\pi}{3}$ as a sum of two angles with known sine values:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{11 \\pi}{3} &= 2\\pi + \\frac{5 \\pi}{3} \\\\\n&= \\pi + \\frac{5 \\pi}{3}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSince $\\sin \\pi = 0,$ we have\n\\[\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3} = \\sin \\left(\\pi + \\frac{5 \\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sin \\frac{5 \\pi}{3}.\\]\n\nNext, we can evaluate $\\sin \\frac{5 \\pi}{3}$ by using its relationship to $\\sin \\frac{7 \\pi}{6}$:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3} &= -\\sin \\frac{5 \\pi}{3} \\\\\n&= -\\left(\\sin \\frac{7 \\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\\n&= -\\left(\\sin \\left(\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= -\\left(\\sin \\pi \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\cos \\pi \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\\n&= -\\left(0 \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{6} + (-1) \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\\n&= -\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\end{align*}",
